## Why This Is Asked

This question tests resilience and reliability fundamentals using real-world API interaction patterns. It evaluates the ability to design concrete tests, leverage mocks effectively, and reason about backoff strategies, idempotency, and rate limits—core skills for building dependable services.

## Key Concepts

- API mocking with deterministic fixtures
- Retry and backoff strategies with configurable caps
- Idempotent retry semantics to ensure safety
- Per-client rate limiting under concurrent scenarios
- CI integration for reproducible, automated testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example scaffold using a mocking library
const nock = require('nock');
const { pricingClient } = require('./pricingClient');

describe('Pricing API Client', () => {
  beforeEach(() => nock.cleanAll());

  test('retries on 429 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(429, 'Too Many Requests', { 'Retry-After': '1' })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 99.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(99.99);
    expect(scope.isDone()).toBe(true);
  });

  test('retries on 500 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(500)
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 49.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(49.99);
  });

  test('respects max retry cap', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .times(4)
      .reply(500);

    await expect(pricingClient.getPrice()).rejects.toThrow('Max retries exceeded');
  });

  test('idempotent retries do not cause side effects', async () => {
    let callCount = 0;
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(() => {
        callCount++;
        return [500, {}];
      })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 100 });

    await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(callCount).toBe(1); // First call failed, second succeeded
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design practical testing strategies for common API patterns involving user signup, rate limiting, and idempotency. It tests understanding of testing methodologies, external service mocking, and CI/CD considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation testing
- Idempotency key patterns
- Rate limiting verification
- External service mocking strategies
- Retry mechanisms and failure simulation
- CI/CD integration testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Idempotency implementation sketch
function signup(req, res, db, emailService) {
  const idempotencyKey = req.headers['Idempotency-Key'];

  // Check for existing request
  const existingResult = await db.getResult(idempotencyKey);
  if (existingResult) {
    return res.status(200).json(existingResult);
  }

  // Validate input and create user
  const user = await validateAndCreateUser(req.body);

  // Send email with retry logic
  try {
    await emailService.sendVerification(user.email);
  } catch (error) {
    // Handle email failure with retry queue
  }

  // Store result with idempotency key
  const result = { userId: user.id, status: 'created' };
  await db.storeResult(idempotencyKey, result);

  return res.status(201).json(result);
}
```

